; lang.xon -- what this bundle IS.
;
; xon: read with the ordinary reader and never evaluated, like every other
; manifest in the x-lang world (pin.xon, engine.pin.xon).  A form a reader
; does not know is a loud error, never a skip -- a manifest that silently
; ignores half of itself cannot be extended without wondering which readers
; obeyed which half.
;
; FORMAT (one form per line):
;   (lang "NAME")               the -l name
;   (dialect NAME)              he | xe | rn -- a REQUIREMENT, not a taste
;   (requires-release "TAG")    the x-lang this bundle was built against
;   (requires-lang "NAME" ["V"]) another lang this one is written on top
;                               of, optionally at an exact version
;   (entry "FILE")              loaded AFTER the dialect, not instead of it

(lang "r7rs")

; XENON, for the same reason x-r5rs is: Scheme's numerics are the tower.  Not
; radon, because the two modules that wanted dlopen -- x/bytevector.x and
; scm/ports.scm -- are not loaded in this release.
(dialect xe)

; PAIRING, NOT PINNING.  A digest freezes bytes; it says nothing about whether
; those bytes still run against a platform that moved.  This row is the part
; that is checkable, and it is compared for EQUALITY, never parsed.
;
; v0.6.0 was the first release carrying what this bundle needs: bundle
; acquisition, `-l` loading, --share-dir answering from any cwd, and eval! in
; the seam.  v0.7.0 is what these specs are green against, and this row tracks
; THAT rather than the floor -- the question it answers is "what was this built
; against", not "what is the oldest that would still work".

(requires-release "v0.8.0")

; R7RS IS R5RS PLUS about 700 lines, and this row is what says so.
;
; x.sh resolves it the same way it resolves -l, arms the required lang's root
; BEFORE this bundle's own (import-path! prepends, so ours still wins any name
; we share), and refuses at startup naming what is missing and who asked for it.
;
; Before the row existed the dependency lived as a probe in run.x: derive our
; own root, look beside it for r5rs/ or x-r5rs/, and hope.  That made a missing
; lang a runtime message rather than a refusal, recorded nothing about WHAT was
; needed, and would have been re-derived by every dependent.  The same argument
; (dialect ...) already makes -- a requirement belongs in the manifest.
;
; A VERSION FOLLOWS THE NAME, and the row below carries one.  It is compared
; for EQUALITY against the version the resolved lang reports -- never parsed,
; exactly as (requires-release ...) above is.  Ordering and ranges would need a
; version algebra, and with it a resolver; equality plus --allow-lang-skew is
; smaller and says what it means.
;
; EQUALITY IS WHY THIS TRACKS THE PAIRING RATHER THAN THE FLOOR.  v0.2.0 is
; where the capability landed -- the string ports here are built on that
; release's polymorphic port source and sink, and against v0.1.0 this bundle
; does not fail a spec, it fails to load.  But only the declared version
; satisfies the row, so it names what this was built and tested against.
; v0.2.1 changed no library file; the floor has not moved, only the pairing.
;
; What it compares against is DERIVED, not declared: `make install` and
; tools/bundle.sh both stamp a `version` file from git describe / the tag,
; because a version row in the dependency's own lang.xon could only be true
; at the one commit that gets tagged and would lie at every other.
;
; v0.1.0 was the first tagged x-r5rs, and naming it is what makes this row
; checkable rather than decorative.  An untagged dependency could not be named
; at all: git describe answers a bare hash there, so any version written here
; would be one no install could ever match, and a constraint that fails for
; everyone is worse than no constraint.
;
; v0.2.0 IS A FLOOR THIS BUNDLE CANNOT RUN BELOW, not a refresh.  The string
; ports below are built on that release's %strsrc and %strsink -- the
; polymorphic source and sink -- which v0.1.0 does not have.  Against v0.1.0
; this bundle does not fail a spec, it fails to load.
;
; WHAT IT CATCHES is the r5rs seam moving under us -- the define mechanism,
; syntax-rules hygiene, the numeric tower.  This bundle is R5RS plus about 700
; lines and reaches into all three; before the row, an r5rs installed from a
; later branch broke r7rs specs with no hint that the cause was next door.
; Now it refuses at startup, naming both versions and who asked.
;
; A CHECKOUT OF R5RS NO LONGER SATISFIES THIS by default -- it carries no
; stamp, only an install or an unpacked release tarball does.  That is the
; intended reading, and --allow-lang-skew is the way through while working on
; both at once.
(requires-lang "r5rs" "v0.2.1")

(entry "run.x")
